keep the game at speed when a present runs long

With SLOT_DRIVE_EMU=overlap (0029) the core runs one frame per tick and the
display ticks once per present. A present that spans two blanks therefore
cost the game a frame, and on the Flip the volume overlay does exactly that:
Super Mario World traced 247 of 600 presents missing a latch with the bar up
against 0-6 without it, and visibly slowed while it was shown.

The display now ticks once for every blank that passed since the last tick,
up to three, and a driven core runs the frames it is owed with all but the
last skipped, so the game keeps the panel's speed while the picture drops
frames -- what the unchained mode always did. A test ticks twice per present:
20 frames for 40 ticks before, 40 after.

With SLOT_TRACE_LATENCY, a present over 25 ms also logs where it went:
advance, step, render, swap. The overlay's cost was visible in the totals but
not in the parts; this is to find it.

Applies on top of 0030, upstream 91499a9.

